<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      260 — drop the account.roles CHECK constraint (changeset 007-account-roles-check).

      That constraint pinned account.roles to EXACTLY 'user' or 'user,admin' — an
      "API<->DB parity" check documenting the two literal values AccountStore.ensureAccount
      ever wrote at the time (the google-admin-emails-only heuristic). It was never a
      security boundary; it was a snapshot of the API's own then-current write set.

      AccountStore.ensureAccount(provider, handle, roles) now SYNCS the native-login
      account's roles from the Casdoor id_token's `roles` claim (normalized to lowercase,
      unprefixed strings — see InternalAccountController.rolesFromIdToken) on every login,
      so a real principal can carry any of the platform's role vocabulary (mod, superadmin,
      matrix, mastodon, forum, wiki, owncast, tube, games, and the *_domainlocked /
      *_external_client variants, in any combination) — not just user/admin. The fixed
      2-value CHECK would reject every one of those with a hard DB error.

      Deliberately DROPPED rather than replaced with a longer enum: the role vocabulary is
      still growing (session.service.ts's hasRole() already matches arbitrary per-service
      role strings case-insensitively) and a CHECK enumerating every valid CSV combination
      is both unmaintainable (every new ROLE_* addition would need a migration here too)
      and unnecessary — this column is never itself an authorization DECISION boundary
      (that's the gateway's Casdoor-JWT-based RBAC, wholly independent of this cached
      session-display field); it only drives which nav items the shell shows. A malformed
      value here degrades to a nav item not rendering, not a privilege change.
    -->
    <changeSet id="260-account-roles-check-drop" author="hugmesh">
        <comment>DROP the stale 2-value account.roles CHECK — the native-login role sync
                 now legitimately writes the full platform role vocabulary here.</comment>
        <sql>ALTER TABLE account DROP CONSTRAINT ck_account_roles</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>
                ALTER TABLE account
                    ADD CONSTRAINT ck_account_roles
                    CHECK (roles IN ('user', 'user,admin'))
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
